{-
   What `@test` may not be, and where it may not be - Design-Test.md §11.2's F1.

   The first of these is the one that matters, and it is the reason the attribute is a diagnostic
   rather than a no-op outside a test build: **a test the build does not run is worse than one that
   fails**. §1.4 is the argument, and this repository has paid for it twice. Every fixture in this
   corpus is compiled without `-test`, so this file is also the assertion that an ordinary build
   cannot be quietly carrying tests it never runs.

   Every form of the attribute is written out below and every one of them is refused here, which is
   the point rather than repetition: what is being asserted is that the *attribute* is what a
   non-test build objects to, and not the shape of any particular declaration. The rest of the
   contract - no arguments, not generic, a result of `{}` or `Test`, and what `@test(7)` is - can only
   be reached inside a `-test` build, where this check no longer fires, and is exercised there.
-}

@test fn plain() -> {} = {}

@test("a display name") fn named() -> {} = {}

@test(aborts) fn stops() -> {} = {}

@test("both", aborts) fn bothForms() -> {} = {}

@test(7) fn notAName() -> {} = {}

fn main() -> Int = 0
